                        "review": "Wayward is far from perfect, but I have come back to play it time and again because it's a fun casual game to play. I'll break it down.\n\nThe Pros:\n1. The pixel art is super endearing\n2. The dev(s) are still releasing updates, so every couple of year I come back to improved QOL, new mechanics, and a better gameplay experience.\n3. I just love a game that lets me build out a farm amidst of a chaotic survival situation.\n\nThe Cons:\n1. Starting a new game as a newbie is brutal. You will die, get frustrated, rage quit, seriously get confused about how sticks work, but if you keep at it, eventually you will have a sprawling base, farm, tames, and maybe even some practically indentured merchants (there's no village mechanic yet, but we can dream)\n2. The UI can be a bit wonky at times. It takes some time to understand how to map the hotkeys and sometimes everything will stop working because you accidentally clicked a tiny icon and didn't realize it was a thing and it will take an hour to figure it out... Wikis and AIs will be almost no help, because there is little written community content for the latest UI releases. \n\nFor a tiny dev team, it's a brilliant build. I seriously hope that others find this game and enjoy it as much as i do. This game has been in my library forerver, so I can't remember what I spent on it, but if you can get it for under $10 and love pixel art, survival, farming, explore style games and are willing to try a new crafting survival that starts with 0 explanation, then get it and give it a try!",
